  "notes": "\"One of the architects of the modern contemporary country sound.\" The key set is documented and unusual for the format: \"the album he is best known for carries F♯ minor, A minor and C minor singles alongside major-key ballads, which is unusually minor-heavy for country radio,\" with a documented harmonic example — \"i–VII–VI–V with a major-quality V (harmonic minor)\" — which is the top family and the harmonic_minor scale weight. Drums are \"live Nashville tracking, 2&4, rock-scaled with 'drivetime drums'; cross-stick verses, big backbeat from the chorus, tom doubling on the final chorus.\" The most transferable parameter is a mix balance stated as a style decision, and it changes the counter rather than the drums: \"mandolin and fiddle sounds … often drowned out by surging guitars — i.e. the country instruments are present but subordinate,\" so the counter runs at a low density and the perc gain sits under the kit. His stated direction style is hands-off with the singer — \"he'll say 'try something different there' but he doesn't tell me to sing\" — so vocal phrasing is left to the artist, which is why the melody here is authored as a register-and-contour envelope rather than as a phrasing rule. ⚠ The bass is \"live bass, kick-locked 8ths under choruses, sustained roots under verses,\" so no `drums.bass808` block. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium). ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc gain and the counter ratio.",
